<h1>About</h1>

<div class="about-lede">
<img class="author-photo" src="/images/amir-khesro-reading-224.jpg" srcset="/images/amir-khesro-reading-224.jpg 224w, /images/amir-khesro-reading-448.jpg 448w, /images/amir-khesro-reading-672.jpg 672w" sizes="14rem" width="224" height="299" alt="Amir Khesro standing at a lectern, reading aloud from one of his books">

<section>
  <p>I am a materials scientist by training, a teacher by heart, and a storyteller by necessity. My journey began in a remote farming village in the Swat valley of Pakistan, and led, improbably, to a PhD in Materials Science and Engineering. I now live in Sheffield, where I work as a materials scientist.</p>

  <p>In everything I thought I knew about my life, an acoustic neuroma was not part of the plan. After my diagnosis, writing became both therapy and purpose. What began as a way of making sense of the experience grew into two books: <a href="/whispers-through-the-fog/">Whispers Through The Fog</a>, a memoir of the journey through a brain tumour, and <a href="/acoustic-neuroma-handbook/">The Acoustic Neuroma Handbook</a>, the practical guide I wish I had been handed on the day of my diagnosis.</p>

  <p>I continue to write about life with an acoustic neuroma here on this site, and I run a <a href="https://www.facebook.com/groups/1209862050611107/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Facebook group</a> where readers and people affected by the condition connect with one another. In everything I write, I am exploring the quiet strength found in adversity and the healing power of words.</p>

  <p>If you would like to get in touch, the <a href="/connect/">Connect page</a> is the best way to reach me.</p>
</section>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>Short biography for press</h2>
  <p>Amir Khesro is the author of Whispers Through the Fog: A Journey through a Brain Tumour, a memoir born from his experience with acoustic neuroma, and The Acoustic Neuroma (Vestibular Schwannoma) Handbook, a practical guide for patients and their families. His journey began in a remote farming village in the Swat valley of Pakistan and led to a PhD in Materials Science and Engineering. After his diagnosis, writing became both therapy and purpose. He now lives in Sheffield, where he works as a materials scientist. He shares his writing on acoustic neuroma at amirkhesro.com, where readers can also connect with him. In everything he writes, he explores the quiet strength found in adversity and the healing power of words.</p>

  <p>Press and bookshops are welcome to use this biography and the author photo above.</p>
</section>
